<sect>intersect (Revision 1.7 -- 09/09/1998)

<p> The unit <tt/intersect/ regroups routines for geometrical intersection problems

<sect1> Routines
<p>
<sect2>line_coord
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void line_coord (const double x[3], const double e[3], 
                 const double dist, double xnew[3]) 
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Calculates the 3-dimensional coordinates of a point <tt/newpoint/ on a line,
defined by an origin <tt/x/ and an unit vector <tt/e/. The new point is at
a distance <tt/dist/ from the origin. (<tt/xnew = x + dist * e/).


<sect2>intersection_line_ell3
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int intersection_line_ell3
(const double x[3],           /* input:  point  coordinates */
 const double e[3],           /* input:  unary direction vector */
 const double axis[3],        /* input:  all 3 semi major axis  */ 
 double *dist1,               /* output: distance from point to first intersection */
 double *dist2)               /* output: distance from point to second intersection */
</verb></tscreen>

<p>Intersection of a line and an ellipsoid. Input parameter are the origin of the
line  <tt/x/ and a unary direction vector <tt/e/. The origin of the ellipoid is
(<tt/0,0,0/) and the 3 semi major axis given by <tt/axis/. 
The distances <tt/dist1/ and <tt/dist2/
 of the intersection points to the origin of the line <tt/0/ are returned. 
The corresponding coordinates can then be calculated using the function <tt/line_coord/.
 The return value of the function is the number of intersections found. 
( 0 = no intersection / 1 = 1 tangent point / 2 =  2 intersecting points).
In the last case the two distances are always returned so that <tt/dist1 < dist2/.


<sect2>intersection_line_ell
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int intersection_line_ell
(const double x[3],           /* input:  point  coordinates */
 const double e[3],           /* input:  unary direction vector */
 const double major_axis,     /* input:  semi major axis */ 
 const double ex2,            /* input:  square value of excentricity */
 double *dist1,               /* output: distance from point to first intersection */
 double *dist2)               /* output: distance from point to second intersection */
</verb></tscreen>

<p> Intersection of a line and a ellipsoid of rotation (rotation axis = z-axis).
Routine uses <tt/intersection_line_ell3/. Difference: ellipsoid is given
by its semi major_axis <tt/major_axis/ and the square value of the excentricity <tt/ex2/.

<sect2>intersection_line_sphere
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int intersection_line_sphere
(const double x[3],           /* input:  point  coordinates */
 const double e[3],           /* input:  unary direction vector */
 const double radius,         /* input:  radius of sphere */ 
 double *dist1,               /* output: distance from point to first intersection */
 double *dist2)               /* output: distance from point to second intersection */
</verb></tscreen>

<p> Intersection of a line and a sphere.
Routine uses <tt/intersection_line_ell3/. Difference: sphere is given by its radius 
<tt/radius/.


<sect2>intersection_line_longitude
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int intersection_line_longitude
(const double x[3],           /* input:  point  coordinates */
 const double e[3],           /* input:  unary direction vector */
 const double longitude,      /* input:  longitude */
 double *dist)                /* output: distance to intersection */
</verb></tscreen>

<p>Intersection of a line and a plane defined by a constant longitude.
Input parameters are the origin of the line <tt/x/ and a unary direction vector <tt/e/.
The plane is given by its longitude angle <tt/longitude/ (in radians). The
distance <tt/dist/ of the intersection point to the origin of the line <tt/0/
is returned. The corresponding coordinates  can then be calculated using the function 
<tt/line_coord/. The return value of the function is the number 
of intersections found. ( -1 = all points on the line lie in the plane /
0 = no intersection / 1 = 1 intersection point).

<sect2>intersection_line_latitude
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int intersection_line_latitude
(const double x[3],           /* input:  point  coordinates */
 const double e[3],           /* input:  unary direction vector */
 const double latitude,       /* input:  latitude */
 const double major_axis,     /* input:  semi major axis */ 
 const double ex2,            /* input:  excentricity of ellipsoid squared */
 double *dist1,               /* output: distance from point to first intersection */
 double *dist2)               /* output: distance from point to second intersection */
</verb></tscreen>

<p>Intersection of a line with a conical defined by a constant ellipoidal latitude. Input
 parameters are the origin of the line <tt/x/ and a unary direction vector <tt/e/,
the semi major axis <tt/major_axis/ and the square value of the 
ellipoidal excentricy <tt/ex2/ as well as the ellipsoidal latitude angle 
<tt/latitude/ (in radians).
The distances <tt/dist1/ and <tt/dist2/ of the intersection points to the origin 
of the line <tt/0/ are returned. The corresponding coordinates can then be 
calculated using the function <tt/line_coord/.The return value of the 
function itself is the number of intersections found.( -1 = all points on line 
lie on the conical / 0 = no intersection / 1 = 1 intersection point / 2 = 2 intersection
points).In the last case the two distances are returned ordered (<tt/dist1 < dist2/).

<sect2>intersection_circles
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int intersection_circles 
(const double center1[2],     /* input:  coordinates of center of the first circle */
 const double radius1,        /* input:  radius of the first circle */
 const double center2[2],     /* input:  coordinates of center of the second circle */
 const double radius2,        /* input:  radius of the second circle */
 double res1[2],              /* output: coordinates of intersection 1 */
 double res2[2])              /* output: coordinates of intersection 2 */
</verb></tscreen>

<p> 2D routine for the intersection of two circles, which are given
by the coordinates of their centers <tt/center1/ resp. <tt/center2/
and their radii <tt/radius1/ resp. <tt/radius2/. The resulting
intersection points are returned in <tt/res1/ and <tt/res2/. 
The return value of the function specifies the number of valid 
intersections (-1, 0, 1, 2) where -1 means circles are identical /
0 = no intersection / 1 = one tangential point / 2 = 2 
intersection points.
